import pf.Html
import ../Generated/DbBench
import ExampleApp
import Heading
import Machine
import TableWrap

## examples/sqlite-vs-turso's results (`DbBench`, from site/sqlite-vs-turso.json, `just db-bench`):
## the everyday run's and the big run's (a million orders, and joins) workloads, each run several
## times in a row on one file per engine. Above each table, buttons pick how many of those runs it
## shows (the first, the first three, all ten: radio buttons, so no script), and each cell gives
## their average as operations a second, a bar against the row's fastest, the time one took, the
## fastest and slowest run, and, where transactions lost to each other and ran again, how many runs
## they took; then why Turso's MVCC reads slower, how it was measured, and on what. Pure: the data
## is the build's.
DbResults := [].{
	view : {} -> Html
	view = |_| DbResults.results(DbBench.workloads, DbBench.big)

	## The sections, or a line saying they weren't measured yet.
	results : List(DbBench.Workload), List(DbBench.Workload) -> Html
	results = |workloads, big|
		if workloads.is_empty() {
			<>
				<Heading id="results">The results</Heading>
				<p class="missing">Not measured yet: run <code>just db-bench</code>.</p>
			</>
		} else {
			m = DbBench.measured
			<>
				<Heading id="results">The results</Heading>
				<p>
					{"Each workload is timed in Roc, in the server, around the "}<code>Db</code>{" calls it makes ("}<code>Time.now_nanos!</code>
					{"), so no HTTP is in the numbers; the app built with "}<code>{"--opt=${m.opt}"}</code>
					{" as it would ship. Each engine keeps one file for the whole of it, as an app's database lives, written and read for a long time: after a small warm-up, the benchmark ${m.runs.to_str()} times in a row. The buttons pick the first run, the first three or all of them; a cell gives their average, and under it the fastest and the slowest. Higher is better; a bar is a rate against its row's fastest, in bold."}
				</p>
				<p>
					{"First everyday work on ${ExampleApp.count(m.rows)} items, each run deleting the last run's and writing them again: what one caller does, then what eight do at once (eight actors told together, timed from the first told to the last done), then a checkpoint and the reads again."}
				</p>
				{=DbResults.series("everyday", m.runs, workloads)}
				<p class="small">Where eight write one counter at once, Turso's transactions (MVCC) lose to each other and run again: the runs say how many it took them, where SQLite's take turns and run once each.</p>
				<p>
					<strong>Why Turso's MVCC reads slower.</strong>
					{" A row it has written stays in its store of versions, in memory, apart from the database's pages, until a checkpoint writes it there (Turso checkpoints when its log reaches about 4 MB). Reading such a row is a lookup in that store and a check of which version the reader may see. A scan through the store costs several times a scan of the pages ("}
					<code>scan</code>
					{"); walking an index and reading each row it names costs far more, and that is how every engine answers the group by: it walks the category index, to save a sort, and reads each row for its price ("}
					<code>aggregate</code>
					{"). With the index ruled out the same group by is a scan and a sort ("}
					<code>aggregate-no-index</code>
					{"); after the checkpoint the rows are in the pages, and the gap narrows (the "}
					<code>-checkpointed</code>
					{" rows). It doesn't close: MVCC checks every row it reads against its store, even a row in the pages, as the million rows below show; and what earlier runs wrote, kept as versions, slows later ones. The recursive query is slow on both of Turso's modes: that is Turso's engine, not its MVCC."}
				</p>
				<Heading id="big">A million rows</Heading>
				{=
					if big.is_empty() {
						<p class="missing">Not measured yet: run <code>just db-bench</code>.</p>
					} else {
						<>
							<p>
								{"${ExampleApp.count(m.orders)} orders of ${ExampleApp.count(m.customers)} customers, a hundred each, spread over the table, with an index on whose they are: loaded by SQL itself from a count (a recursive query, 100,000 rows to a statement, each its own transaction), then checkpointed, so the reads find them in the database's pages, as an app's older data is (Turso's MVCC has checkpointed as it loaded: its log fills many times over). Then orders fetched one at a time and a hundred at a time (by a range of keys, and by the index), joined to their customers, and summed: the ten customers who spent most, and a sum over every order. ${m.big_runs.to_str()} runs in a row, each making its tables afresh and loading them again, on a file of its own apart from the everyday run's: on Turso's MVCC what that run wrote would slow these reads, though it never touched these tables."}
							</p>
							{=DbResults.series("big", m.big_runs, big)}
						</>
					}
				}
				<p class="small">
					{"The engines: ${DbResults.versions}. Measured on ${m.date}, at commit "}
					<code>{m.commit}</code>{", its load average ${m.load} during the run, other work going on: compare the engines with each other rather than with another machine's numbers."}
				</p>
				<Machine machine={DbBench.machine} />
			</>
		}

	## How many runs a table can show: one, three and ten, those there were.
	counts : U64 -> List(U64)
	counts = |most| [1, 3, 10].keep_if(|n| n <= most)

	## A table of workloads under buttons picking how many runs in a row it shows (radio buttons
	## named `id`, each before its label, and the tables after them: a checked one shows its table,
	## by CSS alone). The most runs is picked first.
	series : Str, U64, List(DbBench.Workload) -> Html
	series = |id, most, workloads| {
		shown = DbResults.counts(most)
		last = shown.last() ?? 1
		<div class="series">
			<span class="picks-label">Runs in a row</span>
			{...shown.map(|n| <><input type="radio" class={"pick p${n.to_str()}"} name={"${id}-runs"} id={"${id}-runs-${n.to_str()}"} checked?={n == last} /><label for={"${id}-runs-${n.to_str()}"}>{n.to_str()}</label></>)}
			{...shown.map(|n| <div class={"view n${n.to_str()}"}>{=DbResults.table(workloads, n)}</div>)}
		</div>
	}

	## A table of workloads over their first `n` runs: each one's row, under the engines' names.
	table : List(DbBench.Workload), U64 -> Html
	table = |workloads, n|
		<TableWrap>
			<table class="db-results">
				<thead>
					<tr>
						<th scope="col">Workload</th>
						{...DbBench.engines.map(|engine| <th scope="col" class="num">{engine.label}<span class="note">{engine.note}</span></th>)}
					</tr>
				</thead>
				<tbody>{...workloads.map(|workload| DbResults.row(workload, n))}</tbody>
			</table>
		</TableWrap>

	## What an engine did in a workload's first `n` runs: the average time one operation took, the
	## fastest and slowest run's (nanoseconds), its rate, and its transactions' runs, on average.
	Stats : { avg : Dec, min : Dec, max : Dec, rate : Str, runs : U64, count : U64 }

	stats : DbBench.Workload, DbBench.Result, U64 -> DbResults.Stats
	stats = |workload, result, n| {
		ns = result.ns.take_first(n)
		count = ns.len()
		total = ns.fold(0, |sum, each| sum + each)
		times = ns.map(|each| DbResults.time(workload.ops, each))
		{
			avg: DbResults.time(workload.ops * count, total),
			min: times.fold(times.first() ?? 0.0, |least, each| if (each < least) each else least),
			max: times.fold(0.0, |most, each| if (each > most) each else most),
			rate: DbResults.rate(workload.ops * count, total),
			runs: if (count == 0) 0 else result.runs.take_first(n).fold(0, |sum, each| sum + each) // count,
			count,
		}
	}

	## A workload's row: its name and what one operation is, then each engine's cell, each compared
	## with the row's fastest by the average time one operation took (a rate rounded to whole
	## operations a second can't tell one second from half of one).
	row : DbBench.Workload, U64 -> Html
	row = |workload, n| {
		all = workload.results.map(|result| DbResults.stats(workload, result, n))
		fastest = all.fold(all.first().map_ok(|each| each.avg) ?? 0.0, |least, each| if (each.avg < least) each.avg else least)
		<tr>
			<th scope="row"><code>{workload.name}</code><span class="what">{workload.what}</span></th>
			{...all.map(|each| DbResults.cell(workload, each, fastest))}
		</tr>
	}

	## An engine's cell: its average rate, a bar against the row's fastest, the average time one
	## operation took, the fastest and slowest run's (of more than one), and the runs its
	## transactions took when some ran again.
	cell : DbBench.Workload, DbResults.Stats, Dec -> Html
	cell = |workload, it, fastest| {
		share = if (it.avg == 0.0) 0.0 else fastest / it.avg * 100.0
		spread = if (it.count > 1) <span class="spread">{DbResults.range(it.min, it.max)}</span> else Html.empty({})
		<td class={if (it.avg == fastest) "num best" else "num"}>
			<span class="rate">{it.rate}</span>
			<svg class="bar" viewBox="0 0 100 4" preserveAspectRatio="none" aria-hidden="true"><rect width="100" height="4" class="track" /><rect width={DbResults.tenths(share)} height="4" class="fill" /></svg>
			<span class="per">{"${DbResults.duration(it.avg)}${DbResults.reruns(workload, it.runs)}"}</span>
			{=spread}
		</td>
	}

	## The nanoseconds one operation took, on average.
	time : U64, U64 -> Dec
	time = |ops, ns| if (ops == 0) 0.0 else ns.to_dec() / ops.to_dec()

	## Operations a second: "94,135/s", or to a tenth below a hundred ("1.8/s").
	rate : U64, U64 -> Str
	rate = |ops, ns|
		if ns == 0 {
			"-"
		} else if ops * 1000000000 / ns >= 100 {
			"${ExampleApp.count(ops * 1000000000 / ns)}/s"
		} else {
			"${DbResults.tenths(ops.to_dec() * 1000000000.0 / ns.to_dec())}/s"
		}

	## A time in nanoseconds, said in its unit: "38.2 µs", "1.4 ms", "2.3 s".
	duration : Dec -> Str
	duration = |ns| {
		said = DbResults.in_unit(ns)
		"${DbResults.tenths(said.value)} ${said.unit}"
	}

	## The fastest and slowest run's times, the unit once when both are in it: "7.5–14.1 µs", else
	## "950.2 µs – 1.2 ms".
	range : Dec, Dec -> Str
	range = |least, most| {
		(low, high) = (DbResults.in_unit(least), DbResults.in_unit(most))
		if (low.unit == high.unit) "${DbResults.tenths(low.value)}–${DbResults.tenths(high.value)} ${low.unit}" else "${DbResults.duration(least)} – ${DbResults.duration(most)}"
	}

	## A time in nanoseconds in the unit that suits it: microseconds below a millisecond,
	## milliseconds below a second, then seconds.
	in_unit : Dec -> { value : Dec, unit : Str }
	in_unit = |ns| {
		us = ns / 1000.0
		if us < 1000.0 {
			{ value: us, unit: "µs" }
		} else if us < 1000000.0 {
			{ value: us / 1000.0, unit: "ms" }
		} else {
			{ value: us / 1000000.0, unit: "s" }
		}
	}

	tenths : Dec -> Str
	tenths = |n| Dec.round_to(n, { step: 0.1, ties: AwayFromZero }).to_str()

	## " · 312 runs" where transactions ran more times than there were transactions.
	reruns : DbBench.Workload, U64 -> Str
	reruns = |workload, runs| if (runs > workload.ops) " · ${ExampleApp.count(runs)} runs" else ""

	## Each engine and what it is: "SQLite: SQLite 3.54.1, rusqlite, WAL; …".
	versions : Str
	versions = Str.join_with(DbBench.engines.map(|engine| "${engine.label}, ${engine.version} (${engine.note})"), "; ")

	<story name="The results, as last measured">
		<DbResults />
	</story>

	<style>
		.db-results th[scope="row"] {
			text-transform: none;
			letter-spacing: normal;
			font-weight: normal;
			vertical-align: top;
		}

		.db-results th[scope="row"] code {
			display: inline-block;
			margin-bottom: var(--space-1);
		}

		.db-results .note {
			text-transform: none;
			letter-spacing: normal;
		}

		.db-results .what,
		.db-results .note {
			display: block;
			font-size: var(--size-sm);
			font-weight: normal;
			color: var(--text-muted);
		}

		.db-results td {
			min-width: 9rem;
			vertical-align: top;
		}

		.db-results .rate {
			display: block;
			font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
		}

		.db-results .best .rate {
			font-weight: 700;
		}

		.db-results .bar {
			display: block;
			width: 100%;
			height: 0.35rem;
			margin: var(--space-1) 0;
			border-radius: var(--pill);
			overflow: hidden;
		}

		.db-results .bar .track {
			fill: var(--fill);
		}

		.db-results .bar .fill {
			fill: var(--accent);
		}

		.db-results .per,
		.db-results .spread {
			display: block;
			font-size: var(--size-sm);
			color: var(--text-muted);
			font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
		}

		.db-results .spread {
			opacity: 0.75;
		}

		/* The runs-in-a-row buttons: radio buttons, hidden but focusable, each before its label;
		   the checked one's table shows, the others' don't. */
		.series .view {
			display: none;
		}

		.series .p1:checked ~ .n1,
		.series .p3:checked ~ .n3,
		.series .p10:checked ~ .n10 {
			display: block;
		}

		.series .pick {
			position: absolute;
			opacity: 0;
			width: 1px;
			height: 1px;
		}

		.picks-label {
			margin-right: var(--space-2);
			font-size: var(--size-sm);
			color: var(--text-muted);
		}

		.series label {
			display: inline-block;
			min-width: 2.75rem;
			padding: var(--space-1) var(--space-3);
			border: 1px solid var(--rule-strong);
			text-align: center;
			font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
			cursor: pointer;
		}

		.series label + input + label {
			border-left: 0;
		}

		.series .pick:first-of-type + label {
			border-radius: var(--radius-sm) 0 0 var(--radius-sm);
		}

		.series label:last-of-type {
			border-radius: 0 var(--radius-sm) var(--radius-sm) 0;
		}

		.series .pick:checked + label {
			background: var(--accent-soft);
			color: var(--text);
			font-weight: 700;
			border-color: var(--accent);
		}

		.series .pick:focus-visible + label {
			outline: 2px solid var(--accent);
			outline-offset: 2px;
		}
	</style>
}
